I fell in love with cuphead almost  immediately its hand-drawn graphics  exude charm its ragtime soundtrack  propels the action forward and the  mechanics are as refined as any  platformer I've played and of course  cuphead is also incredibly difficult  drawing on running gun games such as  contra 3 or gun star heroes studio mvhr  prioritizes flawless jumping shooting  and dodging over anything else in  cuphead it's a gorgeous game but if  you've spent much time with it then you  know how brutal it can also be you can  fight a boss 25 times to no avail you  will probably die a lot you may narrowly  achieve victory only when you learn from  dozens of mistakes according to the  creator's themselves that was their  intention from the beginning in a recent  interview with veteran developer Warren  Spector cop heads Chad moldenhauer  described a basic tenet of the games  difficulty keep the mechanics simple  enough to grasp but with enough variance  and technique to allow player mastery  and expression and a sufficiently  skilled player should be able to  potentially defeat every challenge on  the first try no trial by death or cheap  hits in theory this makes sense cup had  amplifies your sense of victory through  tough but fair challenges and you'll  only reach the end through hard work  patience or skill but that's just the  thing when moldenhauer mentions a  sufficiently skilled player what does he  mean what's the metric for measuring  player ability who's to say that cuphead  will eventually click for everyone who  buys and plays it  [Music]  cuphead does provide the option to play  levels on a simple mode bosses have  fewer hit points and will actually skip  some of their attack sequences  altogether if bars less dexterous  players from seeing everything the game  has to offer  what's more you can't play the last ten  percent or so of cuphead if you haven't  beaten every previous boss on the  regular difficulty you can watch youtube  videos yes but you're not playing them  essentially koppad excludes you if you  can't reach the sufficient skill level  its creators intended I myself  thoroughly enjoy difficult games  I adore Dark Souls and blood-borne I  pour hours into XCOM 2 every week  cuphead itself is one of my favorite  games of 2017 but it does raise a few  loaded questions do developers oh it to  consumers to show them their entire game  no matter the player's skill level  should games be made so everyone can  enjoy them can a game be too difficult  for its own good this is a problem  inherent to video games as a medium  outside of ticket prices movies don't  usually bar you from seeing everything  they have to show museums don't test you  before each new exhibit and if you want  you can skip to the final page of the  next book you read most games however  require motor skills problem solving or  some combination of the two if you want  to see everything I don't have concrete  answers to all the questions that  cuphead raises but regardless the game  does spark an intriguing conversation so  I decided to look at how other games  handle difficulty how developers found  creative loopholes to the problem and  how some games turned the idea of  difficulty on its head entirely one of  the more notable examples is Resident  Evil 4 it secretly scales difficulty to  react to the player's skill level  survive longer kill more enemies take  less damage and the difficulty will  gradually ramp up but die several times  in a row and the game will remove  certain enemies the next time around or  lower enemy health the mechanic subtly  makes the game more challenging for  skilled players and more accessible for  those with a casual play style you  probably know about this dynamic  difficulty slider by now but at the time  of its 2005 release Capcom hadn't said a  word about this brilliant system it  wasn't touted and any press releases or  shown off at convention  as the story goes the first public  mention of it was in resident evil force  official strategy guide a year later  this is because Capcom knew the value of  hiding such a mechanic it empowers  players of every skill level it doesn't  shame you for doing poorly and it  doesn't let you waltz through if you're  doing well it's one of my favorite games  ever made for several reasons but the  sliding difficulty is chief among them  left for dead took a similar approach  developer valves famed director doesn't  just alter the types of zombies you'll  encounter or the placement of weapons  throughout each chapter it also alters  the visual effects character  communication and music based on player  status and placement this not only  creates unique pacing in each  playthrough but a particular tone as  well but as clever as these two systems  are they both are reactionary to the  players skill level they gauge your  ability and adjust accordingly in 2014  developer monolith took it a step  further rather than just react to your  performance why not incorporate it into  the story as well why not let the enemy  grow alongside you regardless of whether  you win or lose this idea gave rise to  the Nemesis system in Middle Earth  shadow of mordor enemy Oryx and orcs  developed grudges skills fears and  rivalries based on your encounters  Mayman or a captain and he'll have the  scars to show for it  flee from a fight with a powerful  opponent and they'll greet you later on  with taunts of cowardice different  players will develop their own personal  stories they'll seek revenge on  different captains grow to hate unique  orcs along the way last week's release  of the sequel shadow of war expands upon  this idea  it doesn't punish you for failing in  fact you could say it's actually more  enjoyable when you fail in fighting  dying and fighting again you create your  own stories with our own idiosyncratic  villains waiting at the end whereas  monoliths middle-earth series is about  adapting to failure with an immortal  character others force you to confront  the idea of actual loss two examples  immediately come to mind for access  games XCOM series and intelligent  systems Fire Emblem titles  although you do have the option to  disable it both games place permadeath  at the center of their design lose a  soldier to enemy fire and they're gone  forever  make a poor tactical decision and you  can't go back you make your choices you  try to learn from them you do your best  to move on both series understand that  respawning is a safety net by removing  that net the games become more tense but  also play with difficulty in a clever  way sending your best soldier into the  fray increases your chance for  short-term success but creates more  long-term risk as well if you lose that  soldier your whole team will suffer down  the road you'll need to build new  soldiers up from their beginnings as  green rookies to their decorated glory  days this transparent sort of dynamic  difficulty slider plays with it in a way  that few other games can  I can't talk about difficulty without  touching in some way on Dark Souls  anyone who has heard of from software's  third-person action games will know how  punishing they could be they're  notorious for colossal bosses and  abusive enemies if you don't reach the  next checkpoint you could lose all of  the experience you've fought so hard to  gain but despite their intimidating  challenge level the Dark Souls games and  their cousin blood-borne do provide  options to players who don't have the  same patience or skill as others certain  character classes make the earlier hours  easier while range classes can mitigate  the intensity of many battles throughout  the entire game furthermore developer  from software also provides options to  summon other human players to your cause  or specific NPCs that can help you along  the way the studio made these games  difficult on purpose but also knows that  not everyone wants to lower their  shoulders and charge through the fight  on their own some need company along the  way all of these games I've mentioned  have a common thread they acknowledge  that everyone plays games in different  ways for different reasons some play for  the challenge some play for the escapism  some play to catch up with friends or  just to unwind at the end of the day  some people are less dexterous than  others  some require specially made controllers  to accommodate for disabilities and of  course all of this is ok we're all still  having fun and this all leads back to  cuphead is it good game designed to bar  less skilled players from advancing to  the end of that game should developers  account for numerous play styles when  molding their visions or should they  focus on a smaller subset of people who  want a very specific experience  regardless of widespread accessibility  the larger more loaded question here is  nothing new it's the entire dilemma of  modern art is it defined by what the  Creator wants to convey or by what the  recipient takes from it  shortly after conversations arose  regarding cup hats difficulty rani  ishmael one half of the independent team  Vlambeer took to twitter to talk about  the conundrum of video game difficulty  he outlined the studio's philosophy with  its arcade shooter luftrausers and  described the process more eloquently  than I ever could a lot of people see  luftrausers as a game about difficulty  it isn't what matters to us is that to  you it is overwhelming in the face of  those overwhelming odds the game occurs  the difficulty is the backdrop the  gameplay a delivery method the goal is  Grace Luke Rogers is not about surviving  it's about cutting the engine after a  stall falling down guns blazing pulling  up right over the water  it's about bursting up from under water  through an exploding carrier straight  into impossible odds and a game over  just because it is cool
 

